  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands-CGIAR partnership 2017 - 2021 - ENVELOPE 1</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">During the 2017-2021 funding period CGIAR continued to delver high quality science and innovation to reduce poverty, improve food and nutrition security for better health and improve natural resources management and ecosystem services. The target group were (and are) poor farmers and consumers in the Global South. Apart from a contribution to the CGIAR-wide research portfolio the Dutch contribution made substantial contributions in the areas of nutrition, climate change, policies, markets and institutions, water management in agriculture and forest management and agroforestry. For example the climate change program (CCAFS) helped thirty-six million farmers adopt mitigation technologies and practices or applying agro-advisory services on 69 million hectares of land over its 10-year program. As a result of delivery efforts led by HarvestPlus under the nutrition program (A4NH), 12.8 million farming households were growing biofortified crops in 2021. This translates to 64 million people from these households consuming biofortified foods. In Bangladesh, FISH-led interventions helped protect 264,413 ha through effective co-management in hilsa shad (Tenualosa ilisha) sanctuaries and coastal areas. By applying better management practices for aquaculture, 80,368 ha of pond area were restored. In addition, 344,781 ha of biologically significant areas were placed under improved natural resource management. The Program for Biosafety Systems, supported by the research program on Policies, Institutions,  and Markets, assisted four African countries in developing oversight guidelines for genome editing that accurately reflect the science and evolving regulatory trends for this game-changing, Nobel-prize winning technology.   CGIAR research in one independent evaluation is called &quot;slow magic&quot;. It typically takes 10-15 years for research to lead to innovation at scale. CGIAR research over the decades has generated a median 10-fold return on investment, reaching 25-fold returns in mean terms. The hundreds of varieties in maize, wheat, rice, millet, sweet potato and so on, bred to improve yields and income, enrich micro-nutrients for nutrition, tolerance to droughts, flooding submergence, heat, shorter cropping cycles etc has added billions per year in smallholder farmer income in Africa and Asia.   The Dutch contribution to this multi-donor program has been on average 3-4 % of the total annual CGIAR funding by all donors and projects of roughly USD 800 million.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands-CGIAR partnership 2017 - 2021 - ENVELOPE 1 Partnership between the Netherlands and the CGIAR System Organization (formerly the Consortium of International Agricultural Research Centers), consisting: Contributions to the CGIAR System Organization and selected research programs through a World Bank managed trust fund.</narrative>
  </description>
